      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a bit scary tbh. it feels like these legal terms are just designed to trap you with stuff you cant see coming. like there are all these tiny little traps hidden in the wording that you wouldnt notice until its too late and everything is falling apart. is there any way to spot those 'hidden' liabilities before i sign?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right to feel that way—legal language is often written to protect the drafter, and the \"hidden\" liabilities are usually buried in broad definitions like \"Representatives\" or \"arising out of or resulting from.\" The best way to spot them is to read the contract like a paranoid detective: look for any word that expands who's covered (e.g., \"affiliates,\" \"agents,\" \"successors\"), any phrase that says \"including but not limited to,\" and any clause that doesn't have a cap on liability. Also, check for \"survival\" clauses—those keep obligations alive even after the agreement ends, which is a classic trap. If you can, get a lawyer to redline it, but if you're going solo, make a checklist: ask \"who,\" \"what,\" \"when,\" and \"how much\" for every obligation. And remember, if a term feels vague or open-ended, that's a red flag—push for specifics. It's a lot, but you're already doing the right thing by asking questions instead of just signing.",
